  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how blood vessels, nerves, and surrounding stromal and immune cells interact in adipose tissue, especially around thermogenic adipocytes. Endothelial cells form the vessel wall, with vascular smooth muscle above and adipocyte progenitors and macrophages nearby. The diagram emphasizes extensive two-way signaling between these cell types through secreted factors.\n\nA central theme is the coupling of angiogenesis, innervation, and thermogenic fat function. Endothelial cells appear to communicate with thermogenic adipocytes using factors such as VEGFA, VEGFB, ANGPT2, adiponectin, and HGF, while thermogenic adipocytes send signals back, including EDN1 and NO, possibly also via extracellular vesicles. Pericytes sit along the vessel and are linked to sympathetic nerves, suggesting neurovascular regulation; labels such as NPY and VEGFA indicate signaling between nerves, pericytes, and the vasculature. Endothelial cells also seem to regulate vessel tone through EDN1 and NO.\n\nThe surrounding adipose niche contributes additional signals. Adipocyte progenitors are associated with growth factors including VEGFA, FGF1/2, PDGFs, HGF, and TGFβ1, while macrophages contribute inflammatory and remodeling cues such as TNF, IL-8, WNT, and PDGFs. Overall, the image depicts a coordinated cellular network that supports vessel remodeling and thermogenic adipose activity.",
